Referring to the preceding code block, the do_each_thread() { ... } while_each_thread() pair
of macros form a do-while loop, allowing us to iterate over every thread alive on the system. Some
details on the code snippet follow:
• We follow a strategy of setting a few temporary variables (named tmp1, tmp2, tmp3) to fetch
and set up the required data items, which we then print once on every loop iteration.
• To setup the data we want to report, C programmers often resort to using the sprintf() rou -
tine; note though, that this could certainly become a security issue (due to the well-known
buffer overflow vulnerability and exploits related to it). Thus, we’ve been taught to instead
use the snprintf().

